class.description:
An accepted verdict carries the [NetwIdentity] the session seats the peer under. A rejected one carries the sentence handed back instead. Nothing carries both, and that is what [method accept] and [method reject] are for: each sets [member accepted] and leaves the other field empty, so a result assembled through them cannot claim an identity it also refused. [member rejection_reason] on an accepted result reads as the empty string, which is the absence of a reason rather than a blank one.
[codeblock]
func _verify(peer_id: int, data: PackedByteArray) -> AuthResult:
    var identity := _lookup(data)
    if identity == null:
        return AuthResult.reject("unknown token")
    return AuthResult.accept(identity)
[/codeblock]
Accepting with no identity is a whole answer rather than a half one: it says the credentials are good and leaves the display name to the one the client claimed, which is what a flow that only gates entry wants. It is not reported as a fault. A flow answering no [AuthResult] at all is, because a policy that decides nothing has not accepted anyone.
A flow that overrides nothing still answers one of these, because [method NetwAuthFlow.verify_default] rejects.
